# Habitat Translation for English (en)
# https://wenq.org/habitat/
# Qianqian Fang <q.fang at neu.edu>

# English translation is build-in

# Built-in pages: shown until someone saves a page of the same name.
# The heredocs are quoted (<<'END'), so $names in the text stay literal.

$BuildinPages{$HomePage} = <<'HOMEPAGE_DEFAULT';
= Welcome to Habitat =
<toc>

Habitat is a small, fast wiki engine written in Perl. Put it on any web
server that runs Perl and you have a website that you and your
collaborators can edit from a browser, with every change kept in the
page history.

'''This is a built-in page.''' A new Habitat wiki shows it until someone
edits it. To replace it with your own front page, log in and click
''Edit this page''.

== # What Habitat can do ==

* '''Wiki editing.''' Simple text markup ([[Habitat/Markup]]), a full revision history with diffs for every page, [http:?RecentChanges Recent changes], an RSS feed, and full-text search.
* '''Pages in a tree.''' Names like <tt>Project/Notes/2026</tt> make subpages. A <tt><nowiki><localtree></nowiki></tt> tag lists the pages under the current one.
* '''Write once, reuse anywhere.''' <tt><nowiki>{{Page}}</nowiki></tt> embeds another page, rendered. <tt><nowiki>{(Page)}</nowiki></tt> embeds its raw text.
* '''Page rules.''' A page can carry text-rewriting rules that also apply to all of its subpages, so a section of the wiki can be turned into a bug tracker, a glossary or a slide deck. See [[Habitat/NewSyntax]].
* '''Markdown.''' A page that starts with <tt><nowiki><!-- markdown --></nowiki></tt> is written in Markdown.
* '''Blogs and forums.''' A <tt><nowiki><board></nowiki></tt> line turns a page into a blog or a discussion forum. Every post and every reply is its own page, with paging, tags, sticky and closed topics, and moderation.
* '''Accounts and permissions.''' Registration can be open, or limited to people who have an editor or administrator token. Users can reset a lost password by email. Pages can be restricted for reading, for editing, or locked.
* '''Safe by default.''' Passwords are hashed with bcrypt. Every form is CSRF-protected. Logins are throttled. Raw HTML from users is scrubbed; only pages last saved by an administrator may carry scripts.
* '''Runs almost anywhere.''' Plain CGI on shared hosting, PSGI (Starman, plackup), mod_perl or FastCGI. Data goes in SQLite (the default), MariaDB/MySQL or PostgreSQL. A static page cache lets Apache serve anonymous visitors without starting Perl.
* '''English and Chinese''' user interface ([http:?action=browse&id=Home&lang=cn 中文]).

== # Getting started ==

# '''Create an account.''' Use [http:?action=newlogin Register] at the top of the page. If this wiki only takes registrations from invited people, you need the editor or administrator token from the site owner. Typing the administrator token as well makes your account an administrator.
# '''Edit a page.''' Click ''Edit this page'', change the text, and save. Nothing is lost: ''View other revisions'' shows every earlier version.
# '''Make a new page.''' Write a link such as <tt><nowiki>[[My New Page]]</nowiki></tt> on any page, save, and follow the link to start writing the new page.
# '''Learn the markup''' from [[Habitat/Markup]], and the advanced features from [[Habitat/NewSyntax]].
# '''Run your own wiki''' by following [[Habitat/Install]].

== # Built-in pages ==

!! Page !! What it is !!
|| [[Habitat/Install]] || Download, install and configure Habitat ||
|| [[Habitat/Markup]] || The text markup, with examples ||
|| [[Habitat/NewSyntax]] || Embedding, page rules, Markdown, boards ||
|| [http:?action=index All pages] || Every page in this wiki ||
|| [http:?RecentChanges Recent changes] || What changed lately ||

Like this page, the built-in pages are only defaults: save a page with the
same name to replace one.

== # Source code and help ==

* Source code, releases and the issue tracker: https://github.com/fangq/habitat
* Project page: https://wenq.org/habitat/
* License: GNU General Public License, version 2 or later.

Habitat started in 2006 as the wiki of the
[https://wenq.org/en/ WenQuanYi] open-source font project, built by
[https://github.com/fangq Qianqian Fang] on top of
[https://www.usemod.com/cgi-bin/wiki.pl UseModWiki]. In 2026 it was
reworked for security, speed and modern servers.
HOMEPAGE_DEFAULT

$BuildinPages{"Habitat/Install"} = <<'HABITAT_INSTALL';
= Installing Habitat =
<toc>

== # What you need ==

* A web server that can run Perl CGI scripts (Apache, nginx with a CGI or PSGI backend, or almost any shared web host). For a quick try on your own computer, Python 3 is enough.
* Perl 5 (tested with 5.34 and 5.40), with these modules: <tt>CGI</tt>, <tt>DBI</tt>, <tt>DBD::SQLite</tt>, <tt>Crypt::Bcrypt</tt>, <tt>HTML::Scrubber</tt>, <tt>Text::Diff</tt> and <tt>Text::Patch</tt>.
* Optional: <tt>Text::Markdown::Discount</tt> for Markdown pages, <tt>DBD::MariaDB</tt> or <tt>DBD::Pg</tt> for a database server instead of SQLite, and <tt>Plack</tt> for PSGI deployments.

On Debian or Ubuntu:
  sudo apt install libcgi-pm-perl libdbd-sqlite3-perl libhtml-scrubber-perl libtext-diff-perl libtext-patch-perl cpanminus
  sudo cpanm Crypt::Bcrypt
With <tt>cpanm</tt> on any system, from the source folder:
  cpanm --installdeps .

== # Get the code ==

  git clone -b stage0-security https://github.com/fangq/habitat.git
  cd habitat/habitat

The wiki itself is the <tt>habitat/</tt> folder: <tt>index.cgi</tt> (the
script), <tt>lib/</tt> (its modules), <tt>habitatdb/</tt> (configuration
and translations), plus <tt>css/</tt>, <tt>images/</tt> and
<tt>plugin/</tt>.

== # Try it on your computer ==

  ./runlocal.sh

This starts a small web server on port 51712 and opens
<tt>http://localhost:51712/cgi-bin/index.cgi</tt> in your browser. The
database (<tt>db/habitatdb.db</tt>, SQLite) and its tables are created
on the first visit. Stop the server with <tt>./stoplocal.sh</tt>.

== # Install on a web server ==

# Copy the <tt>habitat/</tt> folder to a directory your web server runs CGI scripts from, for example <tt>/var/www/wiki/</tt>.
# Move <tt>habitatdb/</tt> somewhere the web server does not serve, such as <tt>/var/lib/habitat/</tt>, and set <tt>$DataDir</tt> near the top of <tt>index.cgi</tt> to that path. The <tt>config</tt> file in it holds your passwords.
# Edit <tt>habitatdb/config</tt> (see below). At least set <tt>$SiteName</tt>, <tt>$AdminPass</tt> and <tt>$DBName</tt>.
# Make sure the web server user can write the database (for SQLite, the folder that holds the <tt>.db</tt> file).
# Open the wiki, [http:?action=newlogin register], and type your <tt>$AdminPass</tt> value in the ''Administrator token'' field: your account becomes the first administrator.

With Apache, this <tt>.htaccess</tt> in the wiki folder runs the script
and keeps the server from handing out the modules in <tt>lib/</tt> as text:
  Options +ExecCGI -Indexes
  AddHandler cgi-script .cgi
  DirectoryIndex index.cgi
  RewriteEngine On
  RewriteRule ^lib(/|$) - [F,L]

For PSGI (Starman, plackup), mod_perl, FastCGI, nginx, the static page
cache, and moving from SQLite to PostgreSQL, see <tt>DEPLOYMENT.md</tt> in
the source folder.

== # Settings worth knowing ==

All settings live in <tt>habitatdb/config</tt>, a Perl file.

!! Setting !! What it does !!
|| <tt>$SiteName</tt> || The wiki's name, shown in titles ||
|| <tt>$AdminPass</tt>, <tt>$EditPass</tt> || Administrator and editor tokens (several may be given, separated by spaces). Typing one in the preferences page makes an account an administrator or editor ||
|| <tt>$RegistrationPermission</tt> || Who may create an account: <tt>'user'</tt> (anyone), <tt>'editor'</tt> (only with an editor or administrator token; the default), <tt>'admin'</tt> (only with the administrator token) ||
|| <tt>$PermEditAllowed</tt> || Lowest level that may edit: 1 for logged-in users ||
|| <tt>%Permissions</tt>, <tt>%EditPermissions</tt> || Page-name patterns that need a higher level to read, or to edit ||
|| <tt>$DBName</tt>, <tt>$DBUser</tt>, <tt>$DBPass</tt> || The database: <tt>dbi:SQLite:dbname=...</tt>, <tt>dbi:MariaDB:database=...;host=...</tt> or <tt>dbi:Pg:dbname=...</tt> ||
|| <tt>$DBTablePrefix</tt> || Lets several wikis share one database ||
|| <tt>$EmailFrom</tt>, <tt>$SendMail</tt> || Sender address and mail program for password resets and change notices ||
|| <tt>$UseCaptcha</tt> || A small arithmetic question for anonymous edits and registrations ||
|| <tt>$UseHttps</tt> || Links the wiki builds to itself use https (on by default) ||
|| <tt>$StyleSheet</tt>, <tt>$LogoUrl</tt>, <tt>$FooterNote</tt> || Look and feel ||

== # Upgrading an old Habitat wiki ==

Wikis from the old engine (2006-2023) can be converted, history included:
  perl utils/migrateall.pl /path/to/old/habitatdb/config /path/to/old/db/habitatdb.db
It writes a modernised <tt>config.new</tt> and a new database next to the
old ones, and leaves the originals untouched. Old passwords keep working
and are re-hashed with bcrypt at each user's next login.
HABITAT_INSTALL

# Old name of the install page.
$BuildinPages{"Habitat/Download"} = $BuildinPages{"Habitat/Install"};

$BuildinPages{"Habitat/Markup"} = <<'HABITAT_MARKUP';
= Habitat Markup =
<toc>

Habitat pages are plain text with a few marks for formatting. The left
column shows what you type and the right column what it looks like. To
see how any page was written, open it with ''Edit this page''.

== # Text ==
!! You type !! You get !!
|| <tt><nowiki>''italic''</nowiki></tt> || ''italic'' ||
|| <tt><nowiki>'''bold'''</nowiki></tt> || '''bold''' ||
|| <tt><nowiki>'''''bold italic'''''</nowiki></tt> || '''''bold italic''''' ||
|| <tt><nowiki>`code`</nowiki></tt> || `code` ||
|| <tt><nowiki><u>underline</u> <s>strike</s> x<sup>2</sup> H<sub>2</sub>O</nowiki></tt> || <u>underline</u> <s>strike</s> x<sup>2</sup> H<sub>2</sub>O ||
|| <tt><nowiki><nowiki>''no formatting''</nowiki></nowiki></tt> || <nowiki>''no formatting''</nowiki> ||
|| <tt><nowiki>line one<br>line two</nowiki></tt> || line one<br>line two ||
|| <tt><nowiki>----</nowiki></tt> (four or more dashes) || a horizontal line ||

A blank line starts a new paragraph. A line ending in a backslash
(<tt>\</tt>) is joined with the next one.

== # Links ==
!! You type !! You get !!
|| <tt><nowiki>[[Habitat/Install]]</nowiki></tt> || [[Habitat/Install]] ||
|| <tt><nowiki>[[Habitat/Install|how to install]]</nowiki></tt> || [[Habitat/Install|how to install]] ||
|| <tt><nowiki>[[A page that does not exist]]</nowiki></tt> || [[A page that does not exist]] (click to create it) ||
|| <tt><nowiki>https://github.com/fangq/habitat</nowiki></tt> || https://github.com/fangq/habitat ||
|| <tt><nowiki>[https://github.com/fangq/habitat the source code]</nowiki></tt> || [https://github.com/fangq/habitat the source code] ||
|| <tt><nowiki>[http:?action=index list of all pages]</nowiki></tt> || [http:?action=index list of all pages] ||
|| <tt><nowiki>[#here]</nowiki></tt> then <tt><nowiki>[http:?Habitat/Markup#here jump]</nowiki></tt> || [#here] [http:?Habitat/Markup#here jump] ||

Page names can contain spaces (stored as underscores) and slashes:
<tt><nowiki>[[Project/Notes/2026]]</nowiki></tt> is a subpage of
<tt>Project/Notes</tt>. <tt><nowiki>http:?</nowiki></tt> at the start of a link means
''this wiki's script''.

== # Headings and table of contents ==
<pre>
= Page title =
== Section ==
=== Subsection ===
== # Numbered section ==
</pre>
Up to six levels. A <tt>#</tt> after the opening marks numbers the heading
and adds it to the table of contents, which <tt><nowiki><toc></nowiki></tt> places on
the page (as at the top of this one).

== # Lists and indentation ==
<pre>
* apples
* pears
** green pears
</pre>
gives
* apples
* pears
** green pears

<pre>
# first
# second
## second, part a
</pre>
gives
# first
# second
## second, part a

<pre>
: indented
:: more indented
; term : its definition
</pre>
gives
: indented
:: more indented
; term : its definition

== # Preformatted text and code ==
Lines that start with a space are shown as typed, in a fixed-width font:
 int main() {
     return 0;
 }
<tt><nowiki><pre>...</pre></nowiki></tt> and <tt><nowiki><code>...</code></nowiki></tt>
do the same for a whole block, and no wiki markup is applied inside them.

== # Tables ==
<pre>
!! Name !! Type !! Notes !!
|| Habitat || wiki || written in Perl ||
|||| this cell spans two columns || one more ||
||__ this cell spans two rows || a || b ||
|| c || d ||
</pre>
!! Name !! Type !! Notes !!
|| Habitat || wiki || written in Perl ||
|||| this cell spans two columns || one more ||
||__ this cell spans two rows || a || b ||
|| c || d ||

<tt>!!</tt> makes header cells and <tt>||</tt> normal cells. Doubling the
mark at the start of a cell (<tt>||||</tt>) spans columns, and
underscores after it (<tt>||__</tt>) span rows.

== # Images ==
A link to an image file is shown as the image:
<tt><nowiki>https://wenq.org/habitat/images/habitat_icon.png</nowiki></tt>
gives https://wenq.org/habitat/images/habitat_icon.png

<tt><nowiki>[https://github.com/fangq/habitat https://wenq.org/habitat/images/habitat_icon.png]</nowiki></tt>
makes the image a link.

== # HTML ==
Common HTML tags such as <tt><nowiki><b></nowiki></tt>, <tt><nowiki><div></nowiki></tt>,
<tt><nowiki><table></nowiki></tt> and <tt><nowiki><font></nowiki></tt> can be typed directly.
Anything else goes inside <tt><nowiki><html>...</html></nowiki></tt>. On pages
last saved by an ordinary user that HTML is cleaned (no scripts, styles or
event handlers); only pages last saved by an administrator keep it as
written.

== # Special tags ==
!! Tag !! Becomes !!
|| <tt><nowiki><toc></nowiki></tt> || the table of contents of numbered headings ||
|| <tt><nowiki><localtree></nowiki></tt> || a tree of this page's subpages ||
|| <tt><nowiki>{{Page}}</nowiki></tt>, <tt><nowiki>{(Page)}</nowiki></tt> || another page, embedded (see [[Habitat/NewSyntax]]) ||
|| <tt><nowiki><board type=forum></nowiki></tt> || a forum or blog (see [[Habitat/NewSyntax]]) ||

== # Markdown ==
Put this on the first line of a page to write the rest in Markdown
instead of wiki markup:
<pre><nowiki><!-- markdown --></nowiki></pre>
This needs the <tt>Text::Markdown::Discount</tt> Perl module on the server.
HABITAT_MARKUP

1
